"""
1. Create an OpenSSL Configuration File
Create a file called san_config.cnf (or any name you prefer) and include the following content:
"""
ini
Copy code
[req]
distinguished_name = req_distinguished_name
x509_extensions = v3_req
prompt = no

[req_distinguished_name]
C = US
ST = California
L = San Francisco
O = Example Company
CN = localhost

[v3_req]
subjectAltName = @alt_names

[alt_names]
DNS.1 = localhost
IP.1 = 127.0.0.1

"""
The CN field under [req_distinguished_name] should be set to localhost if you`re working on a local environment.
The subjectAltName section under [v3_req] specifies the alternative names, which can be DNS names or IP addresses.
DNS.1 = localhost sets a DNS entry for localhost.
IP.1 = 127.0.0.1 sets the IP for localhost (or add any other IP address you need).
2. Generate the EC Private Key and Certificate with SAN
Now, use this configuration file to generate the private key and self-signed certificate with the subjectAltName field.

1. Generate the EC Private Key:
bash
"""
openssl ecparam -genkey -name prime256v1 -out ec_private_key.pem

"""
2. Generate the Self-Signed Certificate with SAN:
bash
"""
openssl req -new -x509 -key ec_private_key.pem -out ec_certificate.pem -days 365 -config san_config.cnf

"""

3. Verify the Certificate
You can verify that the subjectAltName has been added by using the following OpenSSL command:

bash
"""
openssl x509 -in ec_certificate.pem -text -noout

"""
Look for the X509v3 Subject Alternative Name section in the output. It should include:

X509v3 Subject Alternative Name: 
    DNS:localhost, IP Address:127.0.0.1
"""